          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and now I am not sleeping. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line, and is the insomnia treated separately?",
          "answer": "Your symptoms of anxiety and insomnia are considered routine in terms of urgency. While there is no emergency pattern identified, it is important to seek medical advice if any symptoms become severe, worsen, or are new and unexplained.\n\nAnxiety, particularly generalized anxiety disorder (GAD), involves excessive worry about everyday matters lasting at least six months and can significantly impact your physical and cognitive functioning (source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder\"). Anxiety disorders are assessed using screening tools like the GAD-7 questionnaire, which measures symptoms and their severity (source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care\"). \n\nFirst-line treatment for anxiety often includes cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) and pharmacotherapy, such as selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) or serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs) (source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care\"). For insomnia, cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia is the recommended first-line treatment, with medication usually considered only if necessary (source: \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\"). \n\nBoth anxiety and insomnia can be interconnected; therefore, discussing a coordinated treatment approach with a clinician is essential for addressing both conditions effectively (source: \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\" and \"Insomnia\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
